STEP 1 -- remove the mirror, exactly as the case is drafted  ............. FAIL
         (DRAFTING ERROR -- the drafted form is incomplete on this platform)
================================================================================
The drafted step writes the removal as `no mirror interface ...` with no argument. Sent exactly
that way, in configure/interface mode:

  x960-56YSQ#configure terminal
  Enter configuration commands, one per line.  End with CNTL/Z.
  x960-56YSQ(config)#interface port1.0.11
  % port1.0.11 is currently configured as a mirror-port. Use caution when altering its config
  x960-56YSQ(config-if)#no mirror interface
  % Incomplete command.

(17:16:15 - 17:16:17 NZDT)

Two separate `% ` lines there, and they mean opposite things:

  1. `% port1.0.11 is currently configured as a mirror-port. Use caution when altering its
     config` is an ADVISORY, not a rejection. The command SUCCEEDED -- the prompt advanced to
     (config-if)#. It is emitted on every entry into the destination port's interface mode while
     a mirror exists.
  2. `% Incomplete command.` IS a genuine rejection. `no mirror interface` with no argument is
     not a valid command on the x960.

>>> DRAFTING ERROR, NOT A PRODUCT RESULT (I-4). The DUT's own help, read at triage with `?` and
>>> no trailing CR, had already predicted this:
>>>
>>>     x960-56YSQ(config-if)#no mirror interface ?
>>>       IFRANGE  Source Interface to use
>>>       none     No source interface (set destination interface only)
>>>
>>> -- note there is NO `<cr>` in that list, so an argument is mandatory. This step is recorded
>>> as FAILING ONLY BECAUSE THE DRAFTED COMMAND IS WRONG. It says nothing about the x960's
>>> ability to remove a mirror, which STEP 2 demonstrates. The case verdict is PASS on the
>>> corrected form; this step is kept in the log as the evidence for the drafting correction, so
>>> that nobody later reads `% Incomplete command.` as an x960 defect.
>>>
>>> The device state was NOT changed by this step -- a rejected command configures nothing.

